    "summary": "Somalia's Supreme Court nullified amendments to the 2012 Provisional Constitution, invalidated the extension of presidential and parliamentary mandates, and ordered elections within 90 days.",
    "content": "Somalia's Supreme Court on Thursday declared null and void amendments to the country's 2012 Provisional Constitution, ruling that the changes were unconstitutional. The court also invalidated the extension of the mandates of the presidency and parliament, and ordered that elections be held within 90 days. The decision, which was announced in Mogadishu, is a significant development in Somalia's ongoing political crisis.\n\nThe ruling came a day after President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud dismissed Chief Justice Bashe Yusuf. The dismissal was widely seen as an attempt to prevent a judicial ruling that could remove the legal basis for Mohamud to remain in office after his presidential term expired in May. The court's decision now creates a clear deadline for elections, which had been repeatedly postponed.\n\nThe political standoff began earlier this year when the federal government and regional states failed to agree on an election plan. In April, the Lower House of Parliament passed a law extending the president's term by two years, a move that was rejected by the Senate and sparked armed clashes in Mogadishu. The Supreme Court's ruling effectively annuls that extension and calls for a return to the agreed-upon electoral process.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the ruling brings both hope and uncertainty. Many in the diaspora have closely followed the political turmoil, which has affected remittances and investment. A clear electoral timeline could restore confidence and encourage greater engagement in Somalia's reconstruction. However, the 90-day deadline is tight, and there are concerns about whether the necessary preparations can be completed in time.\n\nKey figures in the process include President Mohamed, who has yet to comment on the ruling, and Chief Justice Yusuf, whose dismissal may now be challenged. The international community, including the United Nations and the African Union, has urged all parties to respect the court's decision and to work towards free and fair elections. The coming weeks will be crucial in determining whether Somalia can navigate this latest challenge.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-supreme-court-nullifies-constitutional-amendments-orders-elections-90-days/",
